WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.195
Where is the knife?

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:05.508
What happens when the person picks it up?

3
00:00:07.296 --> 00:00:10.312
Understanding a real scene takes both static 3D and

4
00:00:10.313 --> 00:00:11.743
dynamic 4D reasoning.

5
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.200
DynThinker brings both into one open model.

6
00:00:15.433 --> 00:00:19.832
Across nine benchmarks, it achieves the highest overall average among the

7
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:20.900
models compared,

8
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:23.633
including the strongest proprietary baseline.

9
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:25.833
From the same scene:

10
00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:27.567
global spatial structure,

11
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.333
and local dynamic change.

12
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.367
Semantic context gates both priors into the model's visual tokens.

13
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.567
Spatial adaptation can weaken dynamic reasoning.

14
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.033
Decoupled EMA preserves pretrained knowledge:

15
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.400
longer histories for sensitive layers,

16
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.400
shorter histories for others, and online weights for new fusion layers.

17
00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:50.033
In the four B ablation,

18
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:54.367
dynamic accuracy recovers, and spatial accuracy improves too.

19
00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.332
Together, they raise the overall average from 44.0 to 60.3 on

20
00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:01.333
the same backbone.

21
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.767
Static and dynamic reasoning, strengthened together.

22
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:06.700
DynThinker.

23
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.200
Static 3D.

24
00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:09.500
Dynamic 4D.

25
00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:10.600
One open model.

26
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:11.833
Code and weights.
